  "MS cells": `Good morning, everyone. Today we're going to look at the parts of a cell and what each one does. Remember that all living things are made of cells. The cell membrane controls what goes in and out of the cell. Inside the cell is a jelly-like fluid called cytoplasm. The nucleus contains the cell's genetic material. Mitochondria break down sugar to release energy for the cell. Plant cells also have a cell wall, which gives them structure and support. Chloroplasts are where photosynthesis happens. So what's one difference between a plant cell and an animal cell? Write that in your notebook. Unicellular organisms, like bacteria, are made of just one cell. Multicellular organisms have specialized cells that work together as tissues and organs.`,
  "HS photosynthesis": `Let's review the equation for photosynthesis. Plants take in carbon dioxide and water and use light energy to make glucose and oxygen. This happens in two stages: the light-dependent reactions and the Calvin cycle. Cellular respiration is basically the reverse process. Cells break down glucose to produce ATP, which is the energy currency of the cell. Aerobic respiration requires oxygen, while anaerobic respiration does not. Fermentation is a type of anaerobic respiration that produces lactic acid in your muscles. That's why your legs burn when you sprint.`,
  "HS DNA": `DNA is a double helix made of two strands of nucleotides. Each nucleotide has a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen base. Adenine always pairs with thymine, and guanine always pairs with cytosine. During transcription, the DNA code is copied into messenger RNA. A gene is a section of DNA that codes for a specific protein. Alleles are different versions of the same gene. A dominant allele masks the effect of a recessive allele. Let's draw a Punnett square to predict the offspring of two heterozygous parents.`,
  "HS atoms": `Everything around you is made of matter, and matter is made of atoms. An atom has a nucleus with protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons. The periodic table is organized by atomic number. The noble gases on the far right are very stable because their outer shell is full. Metals tend to lose electrons and form positive ions. Take out your periodic table and find sodium and chlorine. Water is a polar molecule because oxygen pulls the shared electrons closer. Count the atoms of each element on both sides of the arrow. A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up.`,
